  <static seed="243" source="Library/WHFreeman/Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition/11_Parametric_Equations_Polar_Coordinates_and_Conic_Sections/11.2_Arc_Length_and_Speed/11.2.3.pg">
    <statement><p>Calculate the length of the path over the given interval.
<me>(9 t^2, 7 t^2 - 1), \, 0 \le t \le 4</me></p>
<p><fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>8\sqrt{18^{2}+14^{2}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>x = 9 t^2</m> and <m>y = 7 t^2 - 1</m> we have <m>x' = 18 t</m> and <m>y' = 14 t</m>.  By the formula for the arc length we get
<me>S = \int ^4 _0 \sqrt{x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2} \, dt = \int ^4 _0 \sqrt{324 t^2 + 196 t^2} \, dt = \sqrt{520} \int ^4 _0 t \, dt = 8\sqrt{520} = 182.428</me>


</solution>
